NetStats 1.03 (03 Oct 2026)
===========================

Network statistics for RISC OS. NetStats shows how much your machine is
sending and receiving, per network interface, with a live graph, plus the
TCP, UDP and IP counters and a list of open connections.

It works with the RISC OS Developments (ROD) TCP/IP stack (Internet 7.x,
based on OpenBSD) and with the older Acorn/ROOL Internet 5 stack. It finds
out which one is loaded when it starts.

NetStats was written because SockStatsLite, by Chris Williams, stops
working on the ROD stack. SockStatsLite asks the stack for the address of
its internal "_tcpstat" structure (Socket_InternalLookup) and reads the
counters from there. The ROD stack does not publish that address (its
counters are per-processor now), so the lookup fails. NetStats reads the
same information through Socket_Sysctl instead, the way the stack's own
inetstat command does.


Using it
--------
Run !NetStats; its icon appears on the icon bar.

  Select on the icon   opens the Monitor window
  Adjust on the icon   opens the Interfaces window
  Menu                 the main menu (also on Menu in any NetStats window)

Monitor
  Current download and upload rates, the totals since the machine started,
  and a graph of the last five minutes (one point a second). The graph's
  scale follows the busiest moment on screen and is shown at the top left.
  Download is green, upload is red. You can resize the window.
  The rates are the sum of all interfaces except loopback (lo0), which
  only carries traffic within this machine. See "Include loopback".

Interfaces
  Every interface the stack knows about: its name (e.g. en0, lo0), type,
  link state (up, full/half duplex, no cable), speed, MTU, hardware (MAC)
  address, IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, and for each direction the current
  rate, total bytes, packets, errors and drops.

Protocols
  TCP, UDP and IP (version 4) counters since start-up, with the change per
  second next to each one: connections opened and dropped,
  retransmissions, bad checksums, packets to closed ports and so on.

Connections
  Open TCP and UDP sockets: local and remote address, state and queue
  sizes. NetStats gets these by running the stack's "inetstat -an" command
  (or "netstat -an" if that is what is on Run$Path) in a TaskWindow, so
  the desktop carries on while it runs and no command window appears. It
  is a snapshot: click in the window to take a new one, or tick
  "Auto-refresh connections" in the menu to update it every 10 seconds
  while the window is open.

Menu
  Info                      about NetStats
  Monitor, Interfaces,
  Protocols, Connections    open the windows; a tick shows which are
                            open, and choosing a ticked one closes it
  Show bits/s               show rates in bits per second (kbit/s, Mbit/s,
                            powers of 1000) instead of bytes per second
                            (KB/s, MB/s, powers of 1024)
  Include loopback          count lo0 in the Monitor totals
  Rates on icon bar         show the current download (v) and upload (^)
                            rates under the icon bar icon, e.g.
                            "v1.2M ^34K" (on by default)
  Auto-refresh connections  see Connections above
  Quit


Choices
-------
NetStats remembers which windows were open, where they were, and the
menu options (bits/s, loopback, rates on icon bar, auto-refresh). They are
saved whenever you change something in the menu and when NetStats quits,
in <Choices$Write>.NetStats.Choices (a text file), and the windows are
re-opened where you left them next time.


Notes
-----
* Counters are read once a second. All totals are since the stack started
  (normally since the machine was switched on).
* The ROD stack counts in 64 bits, so the totals do not wrap. The old stack
  counts in 32 bits: its byte totals go back to zero after 4 GB, which
  NetStats allows for when it works out the rates.
* On the old stack the protocol counters come from Socket_InternalLookup,
  as SockStatsLite did. A few counters only exist on the ROD stack; these
  show "n/a".
* If the interface list cannot be read, the Monitor falls back to TCP data
  bytes only and says so.
* Needs RISC OS 3.5 or later (the windows use Wimp_TextOp and the icon is a
  32 bpp sprite). Written in BBC BASIC; the source is !RunImage.


How it reads the stack (for programmers)
----------------------------------------
Socket_Sysctl (&4121A), R0 -> MIB, R1 = MIB length, R2 -> buffer,
R3 -> buffer length (updated), R4 = R5 = 0:

  4,17,0,0,3,0   net.route.0.0.iflist  - one RTM_IFINFO message per
                 interface (if_msghdr + if_data + sockaddr_dl) and one
                 RTM_NEWADDR (ifa_msghdr + netmask + address) per address
  4,2,6,21       net.inet.tcp.stats    - struct tcpstat (ROD)
  4,2,17,5       net.inet.udp.stats    - struct udpstat (ROD)
  4,2,0,33       net.inet.ip.stats     - struct ipstat  (ROD)

The ROD stack uses OpenBSD 6.8's structures: ifm_hdrlen gives the header
size and if_data's counters are 64 bits. The old stack uses 4.4BSD/FreeBSD
layouts (no header length field, 32 bit counters); NetStats picks the
layout from the stack's version (Socket_Version: 700 or more is ROD).
tcpstat has 64 bit fields part way through, so its size tells NetStats
how the stack was compiled (536 bytes: 4 byte aligned, 560: 8 byte
aligned); an unexpected size is reported in the Protocols window.


